# Database browser

Register a Gloo Repo, and the admin shows its tables:

```gleam
admin.new()
|> admin.database(db)
```

It works on PostgreSQL and SQLite. Registering
[auth](/docs/admin/users) registers its Repo too, so an app with auth can
leave this out unless its own tables live in another database.

The first page names the database and lists the packages
[`howdy/migration`](/docs/database/migrations) has applied. The data pages,
under `/_howdy/data`, list every table.

## The grid

Each table has a grid of its rows. It:

- searches every column at once
- filters by column with equals, not equals, contains, greater than, less
  than, is null and is not null
- sorts by any column
- pages at a size you choose

All of that runs in the database, so a large table is fine.

The grid follows the database. A row your app, another tab or any other
client writes appears on its own, marked `changed` for a few seconds. There
is nothing to refresh.

## Editing rows

Rows can be inserted, edited and deleted. A row is found by its primary
key, or by `rowid` on SQLite and `ctid` on PostgreSQL when the table has
none.

Every value is shown and edited as text, and the database converts it:
PostgreSQL through a `CAST` to the column's type, SQLite by the column's
affinity. In an insert form, a column left empty takes its default, and the
`NULL` box stores a null. In an edit form, every column is written.

A write the database refuses, such as one that breaks a constraint, shows
the database's own message. That is useful here, but it is exactly what a
real app should never show; see
[Transactions and queries](/docs/database/transactions).

## How it follows changes

Each open grid is a [live component](/docs/ui/live-components) on the
server. It runs its query again when the table may have changed, compares
the result with the page it showed, and marks what differs.

On **PostgreSQL**, the grid installs a trigger named `howdy_admin_notify`
on the table it shows. After any insert, update, delete or truncate, the
trigger sends the table's name on the `howdy_admin` channel with
`pg_notify`, and the grid, listening on one extra connection with
[`postgres.listen`](/docs/database/overview#Listening-for-changes), reloads
at once. It also checks every 15 seconds, in case a notification was missed
while that connection reconnected.

The trigger only notifies, and never touches a row. `howdy/migration`
ignores triggers with this name when it checks a package's schema, so your
app starts as before. To put the database back exactly as it was, call
`admin.remove_notify_triggers(repo)`, or run:

```sql
DROP FUNCTION howdy_admin_notify() CASCADE;
```

On **SQLite** there is no way to hear about changes, so the grid checks
once a second. That is fine for a development tool with a few tabs open.
